**Quick answer:** A strong resume bullet point starts with an action verb, says what you did and how, and ends with a measurable result. Use the formula **Action verb + what you did + how (tools) + result (number)**, for example: "Reduced API p95 latency from 800 ms to 120 ms by adding Redis caching and fixing N+1 queries." Keep each bullet to one or two lines, use three to five bullets per role, put the most relevant ones first, and include the exact skills named in the job post where they honestly apply.

Your bullet points do most of the work on your resume. Recruiters skim them in seconds, and AI screening tools read them closely to match you to a job's requirements. Weak bullets describe duties. Strong bullets show impact.

## What makes a resume bullet point good?

{% table %}
- Weak bullet
- Why it's weak
- Strong bullet
---
- Responsible for the backend.
- A duty, not an achievement
- Built and ran the order service in Go handling 3,000 requests per second with 99.95% uptime.
---
- Worked on improving performance.
- Vague, no result
- Cut page load time by 45% by code-splitting the React bundle and lazy-loading images.
---
- Helped the team with testing.
- Passive, unclear role
- Raised test coverage from 40% to 85% by introducing Vitest and writing 300+ unit tests.
---
- Used AWS.
- A tool, not an outcome
- Migrated 12 services from EC2 to ECS Fargate, cutting monthly infrastructure costs by 30%.
{% /table %}

## The bullet point formula

**Action verb + what you did + how + result**

- **Action verb**: Built, Led, Reduced, Designed, Automated, Migrated, Launched.
- **What**: the system, feature or project.
- **How**: the tools or approach, which is also where keywords go.
- **Result**: a number (time, money, users, errors, speed) or a clear outcome.

If you don't have exact numbers, estimate honestly ("about 30%") or use scale ("for 50,000 monthly users"), or describe the outcome ("adopted by all four product teams").

## 30 example bullet points by role

### Backend engineer

1. Designed a REST and gRPC payments API in Go, processing ₹40 crore a month with zero reconciliation errors.
2. Reduced database load by 60% by adding read replicas and caching hot queries in Redis.
3. Cut p95 latency from 800 ms to 120 ms by fixing N+1 queries and adding composite indexes in PostgreSQL.
4. Built an idempotent webhook system with retries and dead-letter queues, reducing failed deliveries by 90%.
5. Led the migration from a monolith to five services, cutting deploy time from 40 minutes to 8.

### Frontend engineer

6. Rebuilt the checkout flow in React and TypeScript, increasing conversion by 12% in an A/B test.
7. Created a design system of 40 accessible components used by three product teams.
8. Improved Largest Contentful Paint from 4.1 s to 1.6 s with server rendering in Next.js and image optimisation.
9. Reduced frontend bug reports by 35% by adding Playwright end-to-end tests to CI.
10. Brought the dashboard to WCAG 2.1 AA, fixing 120 accessibility issues found in an audit.

### Full-stack engineer

11. Shipped a self-serve onboarding flow end to end (Next.js, Node.js, Postgres), reducing support tickets by 25%.
12. Built an internal admin tool that saved the operations team about 10 hours a week.
13. Launched a Stripe billing integration supporting monthly and annual plans in three currencies.
14. Added full-text and vector search with pgvector, raising search click-through by 18%.

### DevOps, SRE and platform

15. Cut cloud spend by $18,000 a month by rightsizing instances and moving batch jobs to spot capacity.
16. Built CI/CD pipelines in GitHub Actions for 25 repositories, reducing average build time by 50%.
17. Introduced Terraform for all infrastructure, replacing manual changes across three AWS accounts.
18. Reduced mean time to recovery from 2 hours to 20 minutes with runbooks, alerts and on-call training.
19. Ran a Kubernetes upgrade across 4 clusters and 120 services with no customer-facing downtime.

### Data and machine learning

20. Built an Airflow pipeline that ingests 50 million events a day into BigQuery with data quality checks.
21. Trained and deployed a churn model that identified 70% of churning accounts a month early.
22. Reduced dashboard query times from minutes to seconds by modelling data in dbt.
23. Built a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) assistant over 10,000 support articles, deflecting 22% of tickets.

### Mobile engineer

24. Launched a React Native app to 100,000+ downloads with a 4.7 store rating.
25. Cut app crash rate from 2.1% to 0.3% by fixing memory leaks found through Crashlytics.
26. Added offline sync with conflict resolution, enabling field teams to work without a connection.

### Leadership and collaboration

27. Mentored four junior engineers, two of whom were promoted within a year.
28. Led a team of five engineers to deliver a new pricing system two weeks ahead of schedule.
29. Introduced a weekly design review that cut rework on major features by about a third.
30. Wrote the incident postmortem process adopted across the engineering organisation.

Use these as patterns, not copy. Every number on your resume must be true.

## Strong action verbs for tech resumes

- **Building**: Built, Developed, Designed, Implemented, Engineered, Launched, Shipped
- **Improving**: Reduced, Increased, Optimised, Accelerated, Simplified, Refactored
- **Leading**: Led, Owned, Drove, Coordinated, Mentored, Hired
- **Changing systems**: Migrated, Automated, Consolidated, Replaced, Standardised
- **Analysing**: Investigated, Diagnosed, Measured, Modelled

Avoid "Responsible for", "Helped with", "Worked on" and "Assisted in" as openers.

## How many bullet points per job?

- **Current or most recent role**: 4 to 6 bullets.
- **Earlier roles**: 2 to 4 bullets.
- **Roles more than 10 years ago**: 1 to 2, or a single line.

Put the most relevant bullets first for each application.

## How to add keywords without stuffing

Recruiters search for specific tools, and AI screening compares your resume to a job's must-haves. So:

- Name the exact technologies: "PostgreSQL", not "databases".
- Put keywords inside bullets that show how you used them.
- Include both full names and acronyms the first time: "Amazon Web Services (AWS)".
- Don't list a skill you can't discuss in an interview.

Our [ATS resume checklist](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes) covers formatting and keywords in detail.

## How to find numbers when you think you don't have any

Ask yourself:

- How many users, customers, requests or records did it touch?
- How much time did it save, per week or per release?
- How much faster, cheaper or more reliable did it make things?
- How many people used what you built?
- What would have happened if you hadn't done it?

Check old dashboards, release notes, performance reviews and Slack announcements. Estimates are fine if they're reasonable and you can explain them.

## Tailor your bullets for each job

You don't need a new resume for every application. Keep a master list of 15 to 20 bullets per role and pick the most relevant for each job. If you target different kinds of roles, keep separate versions, as explained in our [multi-resume strategy](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/mastering-the-multi-resume-strategy-tailoring-your-technical-identity-with-get-hired-ai).

On GetHired, you can ask the [agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/agent) to improve your resume for a job you like. It suggests rewrites, additions and removals tailored to that posting, and you choose which to apply.

## Frequently asked questions

### Should resume bullet points have full stops?

Either is fine. Be consistent across the whole resume.

### How long should a resume bullet point be?

One to two lines. If it runs to three, split it or cut words.

### Should I use "I" in resume bullet points?

No. Start with the verb: "Built…", not "I built…".

### What if my work was a team effort?

Describe your part clearly: "Led the database migration as one of three engineers", or "Designed the caching layer for the team's new search service".

### Can I use AI to write my bullet points?

Yes, as a starting point. Give it your real facts and numbers, then edit the output so it's accurate and sounds like you.

